{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Task Creation APIs in .NET.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Task Creation APIs in .NET.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"task creation apis in .net task creation apis in .net","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Task Creation APIs in .NET.md","fileName":"Task Creation APIs in .NET.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Task Creation APIs in .NET","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 00:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Task Parallelism and Asynchrony]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Task Parallelism and Asynchrony","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Task Creation APIs in .NET.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Task Creation APIs in .NET.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"task creation apis in .net task creation apis in .net","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Task Creation APIs in .NET.md","fileName":"Task Creation APIs in .NET.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Task Creation APIs in .NET","heading":"Task Creation APIs in .NET","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":".NET can represent new work through `Task.Run`, `Task.Factory.StartNew`, task constructors, and completed, faulted, canceled, delayed, or yielded task-producing methods. These APIs do not have identical defaults: scheduler selection, child attachment, state, and continuation behavior can differ.\n\nThe creation mechanism should match the meaning of the operation. `Task.Run` is a straightforward way to queue CPU work, while naturally asynchronous I/O should expose its existing task instead of consuming another worker merely to wait.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Task Parallelism and Asynchrony","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32